      "text": "First, we need to calculate the value of 11! + 12!. We know that factorial functions involve multiplying a number by all positive integers less than it. By learning that 12! = 12 × 11!, we can simplify our expression:\n\n11! + 12! = 11! + 12 × 11! = (1 + 12) × 11! = 13 × 11!\n\nNow, let's determine the prime factors of 13 × 11! and see which one is the largest prime divisor. A prime divisor is a prime number that divides the given number.\n\nWe know 11! is the product of all numbers from 1 to 11, and 13 is a prime number. If we look at how the prime factors of 11! are arranged in terms of modulo increments, we can see that:\n\n- 1! has a prime factor 2 (which is greater than 11).\n- 2! has prime factors 2 and 3.\n- 3! has prime factors 3 and 2 (twice).\n- 4! has prime factors 2, 3, and 5.\n- 5! has prime factors 2, 3, 5, and 7.\n- 6! has prime factors 2, 3, 4 (twice), and 5. (Note: 4 is a factor of 4! but is counted twice since 4! = 24.)\n- 7! has prime factors 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11.\n- 8! has prime factors 2, 3, 5, 7, 2^3, 11, and 13.\n\nLooking at these, we notice that 13 is the largest prime number that is a factor of 11! because 13 is not a factor of 11! but is a factor of 12!. Therefore, the largest prime divisor of 11! + 12! is:\n\n\\boxed{13}",
