      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\), we start by factoring out the common term \\(11!\\) from the expression. This gives us:\n\n\\[11! + 12! = 11! + 12 \\cdot 11! = 11!(1 + 12) = 11! \\cdot 13.\\]\n\nThis means that \\(11! + 12!\\) can be written as \\(11! \\cdot 13\\). The prime factorization of \\(11! \\cdot 13\\) is simply the product of the prime factors of \\(11!\\) and the prime factor 13. The prime factors of \\(11!\\) are the prime numbers less than or equal to 11, which are 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11. Therefore, \\(11!\\) has the prime factors 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11.\n\nIncluding the prime factor 13, the prime factors of \\(11! \\cdot 13\\) are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, and 13. The largest prime factor in this set is 13.\n\nThus, the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\) is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).",
